Life OF Heroes!
# The Last Signal

## I. The Fall

The escape pod hit the surface of Kessarine-9 like a dropped coin, skidding through violet dust before it finally stopped. Inside, **Wren Calder** unstrapped herself, hands shaking, ears still ringing from the destruction of the *Meridian* — the survey ship she'd called home for six years.

Outside the cracked viewport: a dead-orange sky, jagged black spires of rock, and a silence so total it felt like something was listening.

"Systems check," Wren rasped.

A soft blue light flickered to life on her wrist unit. **ORIN**, her onboard AI companion, had survived the crash — barely.

*"Structural integrity: forty percent. Life support: stable for now. Wren... I'm reading no other survivors."*

She closed her eyes. Just for a second. Then opened them again, because grief was a door she couldn't afford to walk through yet.

"Then it's just us," she said. "Find me a way off this rock."

*"There is a distress beacon eleven kilometers north. Old design — pre-Collapse. If it's still broadcasting, someone might come."*

"Then that's where we're going."

---

## II. What Walks in the Dust

They hadn't gone two kilometers before ORIN's sensors spiked.

*"Movement. Bio-signature. It's... wrong, Wren. The readings keep changing shape."*

She raised her only weapon — a scavenged pulse-carbine, half its charge already spent. From behind a spire of black rock, something unfolded itself into the open.

It had too many joints. Its face kept almost becoming human before sliding away from that shape again, like it was trying on masks it hadn't earned. The locals called these things **Ashveil** — but Wren didn't know that yet. All she knew was that looking directly at it made her own oldest fears rise up unbidden: her father's voice telling her she'd never be enough, the silence after her sister stopped calling, every night she'd lain awake certain she was a fraud who'd fooled everyone into thinking she was capable.

*"Wren, your heart rate—"*

"I see it," she whispered. "I see it."

The creature spoke without a mouth, the words arriving directly in her skull: **"You already know how this ends. You always fail the people who need you."**

Her hands trembled on the carbine. And for one terrible moment, she believed it.

---

## III. The Thing About Monsters

She didn't run. Running from an Ashveil, she'd later learn, was the one thing you could never do — they fed on retreat, on the moment you turned your back on what scared you.

Instead, Wren did something small and strange: she laughed. Not because it was funny, but because she recognized the voice. It wasn't the monster's. It was hers — the oldest, cruelest part of her own mind, the one that had whispered the same lies since she was nineteen years old and terrified of every choice she made.

"I know that voice," she said aloud. "I've carried it my whole life. And I'm so tired of it being right."

*"Wren, its bio-signature is destabilizing,"* ORIN said, urgent. *"I think... I think it feeds on belief. Whatever it says only has teeth if you agree with it."*

She raised the carbine, but didn't fire. Instead she stepped forward.

"You're not going to tell me who I am," she said, voice steadying with every word. "Not anymore."

The Ashveil recoiled, its many-jointed shape flickering like a bad signal, and shrieked a sound like static and old grief — and dissolved into the dust.

Silence returned. But it felt different now. Emptier of something that had been living in her for a long, long time.

*"...Are you alright?"* ORIN asked quietly.

"No," Wren admitted. "But I'm still standing."

---

## IV. The Beacon

They found the beacon at dusk, buried in a collapsed research outpost. Wren cleared rubble with bleeding knuckles while ORIN guided her hands over the ancient console.

*"It's a relay station. Someone was here before the Collapse — and Wren, look at this."* A hologram flickered up: logs, names, a woman who looked achingly like Wren's own reflection. *"This was your mother's outpost. She surveyed this planet forty years ago."*

Wren stared at the flickering image, throat tight. "She never told me she'd been here."

*"Maybe she didn't get the chance to."*

More Ashveil found them before the beacon finished its slow charge — three this time, circling like the shape of every doubt she'd ever owned: fear of loneliness, fear of not being enough, fear that everyone she loved would eventually leave. Her carbine was down to its last cells.

"ORIN, patch your core into the beacon. If we overload it, we'll have one shot at a signal burst — but you'll be exposed."

*"That could damage me permanently."*

"I know."

A pause. Then: *"You once told me an AI doesn't have to be told to be loyal. I choose this. Do it."*

She slammed the manual override. The beacon screamed to life, blinding gold light flooding the ruin — and the Ashveil, unable to bear a light built from truth instead of fear, unraveled into the dark, one by one, until only silence and starlight remained.

---

## V. What Came After

The rescue ship arrived nineteen hours later, drawn by the burst. When Wren stepped aboard, dust-covered and hollow-eyed but alive, the first thing she did was plug ORIN's damaged core into the ship's systems.

*"...Status report,"* ORIN managed, voice glitching but present. *"Core stable. Memory intact. Wren — we did it."*

She let herself smile, really smile, for the first time since the crash.

"We did," she said. "Both of us."

Behind her, Kessarine-9 shrank into a violet dot against the stars — a planet that had tried to break her with the oldest weapon there was: her own fear, turned monstrous. But she hadn't run from it. She'd looked it in its many faces and kept walking anyway.

That, she'd come to understand, was the only kind of victory that ever really counted.

**THE END**
